  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$14.70B** of inorganic chemicals (HTS Chapter 28) in 2025, up 1.5% on 2024 but 13% below the 2022 peak ($16.84B). The largest lines are natural **soda ash** (2836.20, $1.47B, down 14.1%), **liquid caustic soda** (2815.12, $1.41B, up 17.7%), high-purity **silicon** (2804.61, $949M), natural **uranium** compounds (2844.10, $836M) and precious-metal compounds (2843.90, $707M). Mexico ($2.20B) and Canada ($1.79B) buy 27% between them and the EU as a whole $2.24B; exports in January–August 2026 were 9.8% higher than a year earlier.\n\nTariffs matter little for most of this trade: Mexico, Canada and South Korea charge nothing on qualifying U.S. goods, and the EU cut its duties to zero on July 1, 2026. **China** is the exception: it adds a 10% extra duty to its normal rates, and U.S. exports there fell 35.1% to $860M. The product-specific costs are trade remedies, such as Mexico's US$195-per-tonne duty on liquid caustic soda and South Africa's 40% duty on soda ash, while U.S. rules decide much of the rest: BIS licences for chemical-weapons precursors such as sodium cyanide and hydrogen fluoride, NRC licences for uranium, DEA export declarations for iodine and hydriodic acid, and a ban on exporting mercury.",
  "latestYear": 2025,
